   "claims": [
    "For example, a deck that is 10′ wide by 20′ long has 200 sq ft (10 × 20 = 200).",
    "Step Three: Determine Joist Spacing You’ll need to know the joist spacing you are planning to use in order to determine the ideal board length and the number of fasteners you need, most commonly 12″, 16″, 20″, or 24″.",
    "Step Four: Determine Deck Board Sizes Most boards are available in 3 ½” or 5 ½” widths and 8′, 10′, 12′, 16′, or 20′ lengths.",
    "For example, if your boards are 5 ½” x 16′, you would multiply 5.5 × 192, then divide by 144 to get 7.34 sq ft per board.",
    "Use the same method to calculate your boards: get the length and width of each step in inches, multiply them together, and divide by 144 to get the square footage for each step.",
    "If you are installing your deck planks on a diagonal or in a pattern, order 20% extra material, as patterns require additional cuts and waste.",
    "As with the decking, make sure you order at least 10% more fasteners to account for any that get bent or lost during the project.",
    "Board feet are calculated by the volume of each board, so you will need to know the decking thickness as well; most deck planks are 1 inch thick, but this can vary, so always check to be sure."
   ],
